  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max and Ilya are talking about a very impressive woman they know. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "В прямом смысле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она умная и красивая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я согласен с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она закончила университет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она изучала управление ресторанами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень престижное образование.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "А потом она здесь работала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она работала в нашем ресторане?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Да, она была менеджером.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не знал об этом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она очень талантливая девушка.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In what sense?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'sense' is enough to make it a question. And Ilya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "В прямом смысле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the direct sense.' Notice the word order: 'straight' comes first, and 'sense' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она умная и красивая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is smart and beautiful.' Here 'it' sets up the idea, while 'pretty' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я согласен с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I agree with you.' A compact expression built around 'I' and 'you'. Ilya answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она закончила университет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She finished the university.' Here 'it' sets up the idea, while 'university' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она изучала управление ресторанами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She studied restaurant management.' You'll hear 'it' all the time in the show, here connected with 'restaurant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень престижное образование.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is a very prestigious education.' Max puts 'this' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'very much'. Then Ilya replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "А потом она здесь работала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And then she worked here.' Notice how the verb meaning 'worked' takes the feminine past-tense ending '-a' here.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она работала в нашем ресторане?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She worked in our restaurant?' Listen for the word meaning 'it' right alongside 'worked' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Да, она была менеджером.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, she was a manager.' Ilya starts with 'it' and pairs it with 'was' to make the point clear. And Max jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не знал об этом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I did not know about this.' Max puts 'I' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'knew'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она очень талантливая девушка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is a very talented girl.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'it' straight to 'girlfriend'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
